{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the people saw, etc.</b> The plain sense is as understood by the intellect. Or, following the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 89a), that Satan came and showed them an image of darkness and gloom, etc., and an image of the bed of Moses showing that he had died. Therefore it says <b>and the people saw</b> the aforementioned visions, and they justified the matter on the grounds that the sixth time had arrived — for that was the deadline he had set for them — and without this they would not have made a reckoning from the words of the deceiver Satan." "<b>And the people gathered against Aaron.</b> One must know the reason for saying <b>against Aaron</b> — if the intent was to speak to him, it should have said, ״And the people gathered and they said to Aaron.״ Perhaps it intends to say that they gathered against him to kill him: <b>and they said, Arise, etc.</b> and if not, etc. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 7a) that they killed Hur, and this is a sign for our words. And the reason the Torah did not allude to the killing of Hur is so that its imprint would not remain visible in the Torah for all generations, and HaShem had compassion for the honor of the people of Israel, and the incident of the calf was written only for the reason that if a public sin occurs, they are told: Go to the public — whose repentance for their sin was accepted, as stated there (Avodah Zarah 5a). Or perhaps the Torah did allude to the killing of Hur from what the verse says (Exodus 24:14) ״and here Aaron and Hur are with you,״ yet in this verse it does not mention him together with Aaron — this tells that he was torn asunder. And the matter is explicit in the words of tradition, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:34) ״also on your skirts is found the blood of the souls,״ etc., as stated in Vayikra Rabbah (10:3). And it appears fitting to give a reason why they killed him: perhaps it is because when he refused to comply, his actions demonstrated that he desired to be an intermediary like Moses — and see the following verse. Therefore they killed him, for they did not want a human intermediary, because in him there is the aspect of natural will, and his forces are drawn into earthiness. And understand this." "<b>Which shall go before us.</b> Scripture is precise in saying <b>before us</b> — meaning, because the YHWH who brought them out of the land of Egypt is hidden and exalted beyond measure, and an evil occurrence might befall them with no one watching over them; therefore they would make a power from among the forces of the host of heaven that would be below, before them. And they never rebelled against the First Cause, but rather they wanted an intermediary. Or perhaps they thought that HaShem had not commanded a prohibition against an intermediary except only as long as Moses was present, and therefore they said <b>for this Moses, the man, etc.</b> And it is possible that a confused argument of error was in their mouths when they said ״for this Moses, the man, who brought us up, etc.״ — meaning, here is your proof that an intermediary is necessary, for HaShem did not bring us out without the intermediacy of an emissary; say from now on that there is a need for an intermediary. And the fact that they called him <b>Elohim</b> is along the lines of what is said (Exodus 7:1) ״See, I have made you an Elohim to Pharaoh.״ And these matters are a great error; and see what I explained in the portion of Yitro (Exodus 20:4)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:32.317702017Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}